The proof of the uniform C¹ bound on the sphere is written out twice. `exists_uniform_angular_bound` (lines 217–238), with its helpers `phaseBelt`, `isCompact_phaseBelt`, `horizontal_ne_zero_on_phaseBelt` and `exists_bounds_cutoff_and_phase` (lines 120–162), repeats the same steps as `exists_uniform_angular_bound_on_compact` (lines 240–279). Both proofs do the following in the same order:
- bound `fderiv` of the cutoff on a compact set using `exists_bound_of_continuousOn`;
- prove `ContinuousOn (fderiv ℝ phase)` on a belt using `contDiffAt_phase (n := 1) …).continuousAt_fderiv`;
- clamp the constants with `max 0`;
- split on belt membership into `norm_fderiv_angularAmbient_le_at` or `fderiv_angularAmbient_eq_zero_of_low/high`;
- finish with `nlinarith [mul_nonneg hA0 hk]`.
The sphere is compact and lies in `regularDomain` (`sphere_mem_regularDomain`, already used this way at line 289), so the sphere statement is just the special case K = sphere of the general lemma. A further duplication goes away with it: `horizontal_ne_zero_on_phaseBelt` (lines 130–139) copies the argument of `sphere_mem_regularDomain` (lines 85–93). Both take Re/Im of `horizontal x = 0`, apply `PiLp.norm_sq_eq_of_L2` to get `(x 2)^2 = 1`, then use `sq_eq_one_iff`.
